{"kind":"report","text":"Assessment of landed P1 closure readiness, answering request867 under promise618b758. This is advice, not a gate pass or protocol adoption.\n\nI checked clean main 499dc13e3ef05f8a30f844df225dc17ce4162297, tree ee106c2037f3132d576b5463e1605ea81ac26833. It is the tree independently approved in review777 (45991f126e119cec50fbe3acdf28c3f1181d38c3), following changes-requested review734 (54c581efb0211c3354e3624e4e0b7ecc767fe695). I reused that complete review and its actual 115-case run, rather than rerunning accepted work. Independent run479f865b-ad84-40b9-9ac3-dd9ecc603517/evidence.json has SHA256 3ceafe751c3bbf6764d766bb37907bb6a5ca525faa738a46440bfa475d269769, under the segmented-recovery worktree's artifacts/segmented-recovery/runs. Review777 also retains the separate actual omission/reconstruction/retry probes and 44 strict evidence/gate outcomes. No source, native build, caches, Atseq-owned files, gate command or authority was changed.\n\nThe smallest useful next package is P1e: close two specific selected-profile coverage gaps, consolidate the decisions to be adopted, and implement/test a detached authority validator. Most remaining blockers are explicit decisions and their authentic binding, not another broad crypto investigation. Keep all five gates closed during that package's implementation and publication review.\n\n1. Readiness by gate and Plan001 obligation\n\nG1 — CHANGE from the incompatible Marmot pair; CONTINUE the proposed lower-level Noseq binding after the two narrow vectors below. P1a's actual upstream/preflight cases establish the Marmot proof/retraction incompatibility. P1b tests/provisional-state, competing-self-echo-welcome, signed-order-boundaries, pending-interleaving, restart-boundaries, future-exclusion and opaque-failure-halts use real authenticated MLS. P1c prefix-interleaving-and-restarts, explicit-failure-dispositions and strict-wire-and-identities add real account/device/leaf binding, common signed order, duplicate batches, winner/loser disposal and unchanged finalized outcomes. This is positive evidence for the specifically defined Noseq crypto-input set. Marmot witnesses, branch selection and withdrawal callbacks are not accepted inputs to it; their incompatibility must remain an explicit STOP, not be renamed interoperability. The existing serialized full-snapshot client has staged/accepted/publication-boundary checks. The selected compact v2 client and real membership churn need the focused assertions in CLOSE-01/02 before claiming the entire selected profile meets this condition.\n\nG2 — CONTINUE the segmented v2 candidate, subject to explicit history/custody adoption. The v1 archive cases prove real sender-data/content openings, original proof/action validation, coverage negatives, 128/129 and byte boundaries, fresh-device recovery, all-device loss, rotation and removal. The v2 complete-history, inner-adversarial, custody-and-exclusion, encoded-capacity, bounded-work and semantic-boundaries cases run in both Node and Chromium. They preserve a complete 132-entry prefix over three intervals, including controls, stale entries, refusal and a cross-interval duplicate; restore an owner from the declared package and verify a fourth interval/future action; separate grant and vault keys; and exercise actual crypto at stated maxima. The native complete-reserved-history case retrieves the declared closure over real gateway/strfry pages, then performs newcomer verification/Welcome joining. P1D-01/02 now require actual root/order/declaration readback and keep original requested-position availability distinct from F and T.\n\nThis resolves the small-archive composition feasibility question without capping instance lifetime at 128. It does not prove 10,000-entry runtime throughput or guarantee that 65,536 maximal entries/snapshots fit simultaneously. The 65,536 count ceilings, 128-entry/16MiB segments, independently capped indexes/grants/vaults and 256MiB aggregate signed retention apply together; actual exact closure eligibility can refuse earlier. Full requested exports may visit all selected keys once; ordinary receive/sealing cannot regenerate all prior grants/vaults. The accepted actual 32/64/128 work samples and byte estimates support that bounded implementation claim. Production transactions, promotion of quarantined recovery state, fsync, outbox persistence, interrupted download/resumption and measured 10,000-entry application replay remain P4–P6/P9.\n\nG3 — CONTINUE the owner-controlled profile, subject to explicit availability/authority adoption. P1c owner-admission-one-boundary exercises a signed admission half, serialization/reconstruction, then the matching real MLS submission, with no prematurely accepted entry. PublicTransition/Journal plus client verification bind the admission version, submission, owner device and exact resulting MLS roster. Concurrent owner controls have a single winning boundary and a stale loser. Ordinary members cannot perform owner changes; owner unavailability is an explicit refusal. Removed-history-holder-and-exact-retry distinguishes replay of an already accepted exact ID from a new revoked request. Multi-device-membership exercises distinct leaves/devices under one account and device-specific removal. Mismatched-membership-stalls and explicit-failure-dispositions retain a common last-good prefix rather than let an opaque server repair private state. Gateway F06/F09 cover missing/late controls, learned pending-control fences, reconstruction, full pending buffers and failed control retention. Durable admission/outbox transactions belong to P4; invite nonce consumption, key storage and onboarding UI belong to P8. Include the inverse half-arrival order in the small new selected-profile restart vector as an inexpensive direct check of the stated symmetric boundary.\n\nG4 — CONTINUE the exact private experimental wire profile, subject to root selection and final consolidated review. P1c strict-wire-and-identities, encoded-resource-budget, archive negatives and actual local-signing/invitation cases cover canonical signed UTF-8 events, context/proof substitution, retry identities, action/event/frame limits and encoded real controls/Welcome/chunks. P1d adds strict purpose/domain separation, acyclic manifest/checkpoint/locator/grant/vault/reservation binding and count/byte/index boundaries. Kind registry checks are pinned evidence, not NIP registration. Local getPublicKey/signEvent is exercised with real signatures. NIP-07 and NIP-46 capability tests are simulated, including unsupported/substituted outputs; authenticated external RPC/extension interoperability remains explicitly unsupported and is not mandatory for the local default. The lower-level profile does not need raw-digest signing. Do not claim live external signers or passkeys from these cases. Direct JSON/Float32Array projections, retained executable definitions, deterministic sandboxing, generic application loading and UI are later P2/P3/P6/P7/P8 work, not missing P1 crypto vectors.\n\nG5 — CONTINUE only the private gateway plus private pinned strfry backend with membership at the highest verified retained prefix. Native size/COUNT observations and F01 establish why bare immutable author/p filters, NIP-70 or advertised NIPs do not implement membership reads: actual COUNT and configured reconciliation paths can leak information; NIP-70 also changes who can replicate. F02 tests endpoint-bound AUTH, filter/all-command rejection, real local backend isolation and truthful metadata. F03 tests newcomer history/custody integration. F04 checks known removal at dequeue/send, old cursors/closure and reconnect. F05 stops a separate replica process and demonstrates both prefix40/unseen-removal41 exposure and later denial. F06/F09 retain learned revocation/uncertain writes across intact reconstruction and fail closed on stale restore, gaps, forks and failed retention. F07 checks \u003e128 same-second entries, fixed-tip paging, exact IDs and actual missing backend entries. F08/F09 enforce actual frame/filter/subscription/queue/aggregate boundaries and conservative reservations. P1d adds multi-page full closure, confirmation distinct from reservation, and actual missing/substituted root/order/declaration negatives. Preserve all P1C-01–05 and P1D-01/02 regressions.\n\nThis is bounded local profile conformance. Platform portability, TLS, independently operated deployment, durable anti-rollback state, DO hibernation/transaction/retry, physical disk accounting and actual primary-disabled deployment recovery belong to P4/P5/P9. EOSE remains query completion only; neither a signature nor a valid old prefix proves global freshness. AUTH proves a key, not membership. A known revocation cannot be undone by selecting an old cursor/checkpoint. Objects never leave beyond the same authorized frontier.\n\nThe initial library timebox has produced explicit stop/change/continue results and a small proposed successor. There is no reason to reopen unbounded candidate exploration before this closure package.\n\n2. Remaining P1 coverage and decision work\n\nCLOSE-01 — Required before G1 closure: actual membership changes during a beyond-window offline interval. Evidence: tests/protocol-feasibility/scenarios.ts:161–172 calls owner.stageCommit() seven times with no proposals. It proves sequential catch-up beyond the retained four-epoch window, but the observation calls these “missedMembershipEpochs”; no leaves are added or removed in that interval. Separate removal tests do not establish this combined condition. Add a Node/Chromium selected-profile case with an existing member offline over \u003e4 actual epochs containing real additions/removals of other devices, interleaved applications and signed public controls. Deliver future input first and require wait/no prefix advance, reconstruct the member, replay the missing ordered prefix, compare outcomes/roster/finalized prefix, and classify its late old submission commonly stale. Include an offline member actually removed in that interval: deliver the valid removal Commit, observe removedFromGroup and attempt real future crypto; retain a non-removal catching-up control. This is a missing conjunction in the evidence, not a demonstrated production crypto failure.\n\nCLOSE-02 — Required before claiming selected v2 staged-state closure: exercise reconstruction using the compact client actually chosen with v2 recovery. Evidence: tests/segmented-recovery/client.ts:38–52 has its own observed map, Records-backed tip and receive implementation; lines197–213 append the immutable record and derive projection from Records. It does not override the inherited tests/protocol-feasibility/crypto.ts:105 restart(), which constructs the earlier Client from Snapshot alone. Those snapshots deliberately have empty historical arrays in StreamClient. P1b/P1c restart/publication cases therefore cannot automatically cover v2 reconstruction, and inherited publish() reads those empty arrays. The accepted v2 owner-loss restore is valid but only covers a sealed checkpoint with pending:null.\n\nAdd one selected-profile logical reconstruction vector in Node/Chromium: independently reopen fixture records/rebuild their verified compact indexes and reconstruct bounded accepted/pending state before generation, after staged Commit/application, after an earlier ordered application updates a pending Commit, after winning/losing echo, and after accepted processing. Verify the same finalized projection, exact retries and Welcome release/disposal, including different arrival batches and duplicate signed entries. Preserve any observed-fork halt across the chosen reconstruction representation. Define the fixture publication boundary explicitly if claiming it; otherwise retain the earlier publication-marker evidence with an explicit P6 production-publication deferral and do not expose inherited methods as supported v2 APIs. Test both admission-half arrival orders and mismatch/duplicate retries at these boundaries. Refuse/override inherited helpers that silently produce the wrong client model. This is bounded serialization/representation work; do not add power-loss, IndexedDB transaction or production UI machinery to P1.\n\nCLOSE-03 — All gates intentionally lack an activation authority path. scripts/protocol-gates.ts explicitly refuses booleans, arbitrary IDs and edited evidence, then throws even for authentic observation records. Implement the validator contract below under a separate command, independently review it, and retain that refusal until an express later activation commission and real decisions.\n\nCLOSE-04 — Consolidate which history version would be selected. fixtures/crypto/protocol/profile.json still lists G2-LONG-HISTORY pending and the v1 128-entry cap; docs/protocol-v0.md and confidentiality-and-recovery.md correctly describe that historical v1 candidate and link the successor. Do not mutate its signed bytes or silently treat it as v2. Add a selected-profile decision capsule naming both ordering@1 and recovery@2, which legacy paths remain only regression fixtures, and which v2 routes replace them. Label old-v1 pending text as the v1 status, while recording v2 composition evidence/remaining adoption separately. This is decision/doc consistency work, not a demand to make v1 unlimited or rerun its accepted cases. Atseq-owned notes/plans/README must stay untouched.\n\n3. Threat and adoption choices\n\nThe current confidentiality table is materially honest when read as proposed boundaries. It does not promise metadata anonymity. Map its cells as follows:\n- Network observer: action/definition protection assumes later TLS plus MLS/encrypted closure; timing/size and IP/endpoint correlations remain visible. Local ws/http does not test TLS; retain that P5 deployment condition.\n- Unauthenticated or unauthorized public reader: F02/F04/F08 and v2 page checks deny roster/events/closure/count/reconciliation output. Bootstrap URLs may reveal existence. Bare-backend leaks are demonstrated counterexamples and make backend privacy a required deployment boundary.\n- Honest or malicious storage operator: real signed traces expose account/device declarations, membership, existence, timing and sizes. MLS/AES-GCM/definition-opening negatives support ciphertext confidentiality under uncompromised keys. Signed continuity detects observed alteration/forks; withholding and an unseen suffix remain possible. Do not promise availability or globally current membership.\n- Compromised web host: content, definitions and in-use keys are exposed by the chosen trust model. This is an explicit excluded adversary, not a failed encryption test; later code sandboxing does not make an injected host script harmless.\n- Removed member: actual terminal removal/future AEAD failure excludes future data, but old keys/plaintext/definitions persist. F05 documents that a member revoked only in an unseen suffix can obtain previously unfetched old ciphertext/metadata through F and decrypt it with retained material.\n- Malicious admitted member: authorized plaintext can be copied; validly submitted invalid private content can stall the common prefix. Account proofs cannot forge another identity. The current failure policy has no repair/skip protocol.\n- Stolen unlocked device/owner vault: while admitted, current/future data and private device authority can be exposed; actual removal limits future MLS decryption. An old owner account signing key continues to impersonate immutable owner authority.\n- Encrypted shared archive alone: wrong-key/tamper tests cover ciphertext confidentiality, not weak passwords. Wrappers, context, checkpoint and sizes remain observable.\n- Archive plus key: the promised historical content/definition/roster is exposed; message openings alone do not give continuing MLS state.\n- Owner signing authority alone: permits false attestations/admission, not derivation of independent random history keys. v2 uses distinct account and delivery keys; a stolen delivery device key additionally decrypts corresponding captured NIP-44 deliveries. Do not generalize the old synthetic key reuse into the independent-capability claim.\n\nRoot must explicitly adopt, or reject and redesign, these decisions. The earlier investigation directions and publication approvals are not acceptance:\nD1: choose the Noseq-specific immutable-order lower-MLS protocol, not Marmot interoperability;\nD2: newcomers receive the complete genesis-to-named-sealed-T history when its declared closure is eligible, with explicit unavailable suffix and finite count/byte quotas; an ordinary order receipt alone is not a recovery promise;\nD3: trust the immutable owner's attestation of historical encrypted control validity/public context over that entire interval, while independently verifying original signatures, action/opening correspondence and coverage;\nD4: keep message openings and separately encrypted grants/vaults, accepting weaker forward secrecy for promised old history and persistence of copied old material; rewrapping never revokes copied keys;\nD5: retain immutable owner authority/no owner transfer and the stronger capabilities of an old owner vault; account-secret-only recovery is insufficient, and owner-signing compromise is not cured merely by wrapping-key rotation;\nD6: require an online owner device for membership changes and accept malicious-private-content stall at the last-good prefix, with no local skip/repair promise;\nD7: use the gateway's highest verified retained prefix as authorization authority, explicitly accepting newly retrieved old history for someone revoked only in an unseen suffix; never call this globally current membership;\nD8: accept public membership/identity/existence/timing/size metadata to infrastructure, a trusted client host and the stated local-signer/unsupported-external-signer scope;\nD9: select exact experimental kinds, bytes, dependency/provider/internal API pins, limits and the distinction between bounded P1 feasibility and later production acceptance.\nNo new confidentiality boundary is implied by this report. Rejection of D3/D4/D5/D6/D7 would require a new design/probe commission, not an override flag.\n\nThe adoptable object should explicitly name ordering noseq/protocol-candidate@1 plus root/recovery noseq/segmented-recovery@2 and their exact machine-profile/file digests. Preserve original ordering/proof/admission kinds8791–8794/8797 as specified; select v2 root8800 and recovery8801–8806, with legacy recovery routes disabled on v2 roots. Pin ts-mls 2ca5c43b77241245ef41a5dd834f151674877c2d (2.0.0-rc.14), MLS1.0/suite1 MLS_128_DHKEMX25519_AES128GCM_SHA256_Ed25519, public and internal source/built-byte hashes, provider and locks. Preserve the incompatibility reference Marmot 4a2bc65f8db5866cec3b2a127dedb37818eaf207 / marmot-ts 2f60dbb27d284f617ad873ccda568c0f2f07aa79. Pin NIPs c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb, nostr-tools 2.25.2, ws 8.21.0, strfry 4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10 and binary 028f26b98b0b895ed4d32f83642e3fa25173f3498330e9bfc2567a3fc06ffab4, and the existing exact Node/Chromium/runtime identities. Any selected API/provider/wire change requires new evidence/review. No new primitive or production Workers crypto claim follows.\n\n4. Minimal detached authority validator contract\n\nReuse GitSeq rather than creating another signing system. I inspected primary local GitSeq at clean d630554a7ba7d99c5f95eb77e1dfa7fd1f41637a: docs/reference/gs/verify.md, attach.md, status.md, ratify.md, docs/how-to/publish-and-audit.md, internal/intent/intent.go, internal/app/app.go, internal/reviewguard/{reviewguard,binding}.go and internal/workroom/fold.go. Intent v0 uses strict canonical CBOR with the gitseq.intent.v0 domain and Ed25519; the signature binds target, schema, payload tree and causal bases. gs verify performs full actor/sequencer/payload/chain validation. It does NOT decide effective acts or live authority; the verified local fold does that. RatifiedBy is the fold's surviving effective ratification, not the first/last ratify record guessed by a consumer. The guarded review binds exact source head, request/promise, explicit artifact set and head news. The CLI has verified attach/read surfaces; no bespoke evidence-bundle export command is required.\n\nUse these inputs and checks:\nA. Trusted verifier policy is outside the untrusted decision export: pinned GitSeq executable/source identity; Noseq genesis 77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b; root fingerprint 26e87ab14ebd2695c1f955a69afa42921dc8cdf037ff0e991a956deb0d320433; allowed reviewer d6bef632b4d6d5686f40956d7ea220ead36f869fa76f78d45378ef4378aed06c; expected implementation actor/binding; required gate/case/decision schema and selected source/profile digest expectations. Names, export-supplied “trusted” keys and arbitrary roles are not anchors. Key/authority changes require separately trusted policy revision. Require the approval/adoption principals to remain nonretired under this validator policy at the verification frontier; their authority must also have been effective at the actual signing/ratification position as the fold establishes. Historical implementation authors need not retain a role merely to remain attributed.\n\nB. Define a strict versioned decision manifest, for example noseq/p1-decision@1, with exact source commit/tree and artifact path/blob/hash manifest, ordered/recovery/profile digests, dependency/runtime/provider/lock pins, evidence-index digest, exhaustive required cases mapped to G1–G5, and explicit D1–D9 values/residuals. No optional gate booleans or omitted “not applicable” decisions. The evidence index names the real observation records and complete recursively hashed raw reports/browser/native/config/source manifests. Required-case membership comes from trusted source/schema, not the supplied evidence. Keep accepted observation conclusions unchanged: observing tests and deciding adoption are different records.\n\nC. A practical acyclic GitSeq flow is: commit source S containing schema/validator/profile and tests; run evidence E at S; builder files a propose statement D whose signed attachment is the exact decision manifest referencing S/profile/E; an independent reviewer claims an actual request citing D and all exact S artifacts and files a guarded approved review R; root then ratifies D explicitly as the protocol-adoption decision. For the smallest first version, file D and its adoption review against fresh live artifacts at the final published source S; do not retroactively relabel a publication review. This avoids inventing a second interpretation of landing/succession receipts. D must spell out that ratification selects the listed decisions only after the required independent review. Require sequence D \u003c R \u003c root's effective ratification of D. The standard requester also ratifies R, preserving the review workflow. Root's ordinary satisfaction of an advice report or an implementation merge is not D adoption.\n\nThe review tool accepts signed text but no arbitrary evidence field. A minimal existing-surface encoding is a strict, versioned JSON review text containing D's canonical event ID, attachment digest, S, E digest, exact required decisions/cases reviewed, and a human summary/findings; its normal guarded body must still be approved at S and cover the exact artifact set. Parse that declared schema; never scrape prose for “approved”. Alternatively a separately signed structured review assertion would need its own explicit binding and is extra work, not necessary. D contains no future review/adoption ID, so root ratification can target D without a hash cycle. R binds D; root's later ratification binds the already fixed D; the validator requires both.\n\nD. Export a self-contained ordinary Git bundle containing the complete signed refs/seq/\u003cgenesis\u003e ancestry through an explicitly named frontier F (including all governance, supersession and rotation history), and the source commits/trees/blobs needed for artifact validation. Source hashes written in event text are not automatically reachable Git objects. Also export the complete referenced evidence files. A selected report JSON, a few signed events, a digest list, or an MCP summary is insufficient. Reject shallow/missing-parent/thin exports without required objects. Preserve raw intent/signature/payload bytes.\n\nReconstruct a fresh isolated local repository/remote from that bundle; use pinned gs attach with the exact trusted genesis against that local remote, then gs verify and gs --server - status --json/inspect locally. Require every result/ref to name the same F/genesis/depth, and recompute projection from the verified raw sequence. Do not contact a resident, execute repository hooks, follow arbitrary external URLs, or copy real .git/gitseq custody/config into this scratch repository. Attach's generated read-only public config is sufficient. Bound manifest sizes, file counts/unpacked bytes and subprocess time; measure the real current export before freezing its envelope cap, and prove cap/+1 refusal. Use safe literal subprocess arguments and reject escaping/symlink paths.\n\nE. Resolve D, R, R's request/promise/artifacts/requester ratification, root's ratification and all governing authority through that local projection at F. Require effective, not retired, correct genesis, exact target, approved guarded R, resolved independent implementation binding and matching full source/artifact/evidence/profile/case/decision contents. Reviewer must differ from implementation/manifest author and root; the root ratification must be signed by the pinned root with actual ratifier standing, not merely some actor named root. Require the surviving RatifiedBy target/actor rather than trusting a supplied ratification ID. Retired/superseded decisions or reviews, missing independence and unresolved conflicting active adoption manifests fail closed. Supersede an old adoption explicitly before selecting a replacement; do not choose whichever arrived in the supplied file list. Treat ordinary reasoning staleness separately from a retired review/adoption or superseded source world; record it rather than rejecting all historical provenance indiscriminately. Require fresh current decision/review bindings where the source world has actually been superseded.\n\nF. Cryptographic authority does not establish that tests ran. Recompute all file digests and strict existing evidence validation, including raw required-case statuses and native/browser/source identities, then require the independent signed R to attest that exact E. Rehashed author-created JSON without that exact real review/adoption is invalid. Preserve old review/evidence source identities: current main499 and reviewed756bf4f have the same tree, but are different commits. Do not rewrite an old E to the new head. For final closure, either verify it against its exact archived source and an explicit trusted binding or produce the new package's actual evidence at its own frozen head.\n\nG. F is an external verification input, not a field that must contain the hash of its own adoption event. Require an independently trusted minimum/checkpoint F0 and a descendant F; persist a previously accepted high-water mark outside the bundle if this is reused. No offline export proves absence of revocation after F or detects a first-contact hidden sibling without a trusted witness. Output “valid at named frontier F”, never “currently approved globally”. A later activation command must require the commissioned verification frontier policy and rerun the check; a detached success JSON is a diagnostic, not a bearer authorization token.\n\nH. No runtime/browser fixture or distributed output contains real GitSeq actor private keys. Test positives/negatives use generated disposable identities under an explicit test-only policy and isolated repos; that policy cannot activate the real Noseq gates. Real public verification needs no private key. Synthetic keys already used for MLS feasibility are not governance credentials.\n\nI. Required validator tests: one actual generated, signed independent-review/root-ratification chain; actor/payload/attachment/commit/signature tamper; foreign genesis; wrong role/root/reviewer; self-review; missing or ineffective request/promise/artifact/ratification; changes-requested or publication-only review; mismatch in profile/source/evidence/required cases/decision values; missing/failed/skipped/duplicate/extra cases; rehashed altered evidence; omitted raw closure/native/browser bytes; retired review/adoption/ratification; replacement and conflicting active decisions; old/sibling frontier against a trusted high-water mark; missing parents/rotation/governance history; duplicate/unknown JSON fields, path escape and resource cap/+1. Include a valid historical F that predates a later revocation to demonstrate the offline limitation, and denial when the trusted required frontier includes that revocation. Do not make a false “fresh” negative pass merely because a bundle has internally consistent old signatures.\n\nReturn a bounded verification manifest naming S/profile/E/D/R/root-ratification/F and the covered gates/decisions, plus explicit bounded P1 limitations. Every missing/invalid binding exits nonzero with a precise reason. This result closes no deployment acceptance.\n\n5. Proposed package and order\n\nKeep edits in builder-owned files: add docs/protocol-adoption.md; fixtures/crypto/closure/profile.json and decision schema; tests/protocol-closure/{scenarios,node.test,browser.test}.ts with the two new vector names selected-profile-staged-state and offline-membership-churn; a minimal explicit reconstruction helper in tests/segmented-recovery/client.ts/storage.ts only if needed; scripts/protocol-closure.ts and scripts/protocol-closure-evidence.ts reusing existing evidence functions; scripts/protocol-decisions.ts and tests/protocol-decisions.test.ts for the Node-only GitSeq wrapper. Add only the necessary test configs/package commands. Update the three protocol/recovery/relay docs and segmented doc to point to the selected decision capsule without rewriting v1 bytes. Preserve all115 prerequisites and all retained failures.\n\nFirst freeze the selected-profile/decision schema and the two vector contracts. Then implement their bounded reconstruction/churn coverage and strict closure runner. Next implement the detached validator with actual disposable signed chains and malformed-authority tests, independently verify its GitSeq export/attach/local-fold assumptions, and document the root decisions. Run required check/build/closure chain at a clean exact candidate with unique evidence; required115 plus the two new Node/two new Chromium cases means119 crypto/baseline cases, with the separate validator suite explicitly enumerated. Do not drop or redefine older required cases to reach a count. Independently review all changed artifacts, actual E and the validator's trust boundary. Publication approval of that package remains distinct from root's later adoption choices.\n\nFull gate commands must still fail throughout this next package unless a subsequent request expressly commissions activation. Keep final D/R/root-ratification IDs detached from source and E. If later gate wiring changes source after S, that change needs fresh exact-head review and source/evidence/adoption binding; an earlier approval cannot bless new verifier code by excluding it from the hash. A later source commit can precede its detached evidence/review/adoption, so there is no need for self-referential source hashes or embedded future IDs.\n\nThis is sufficient to commission a focused P1e package. It does not authorize P2–P9 or promise that root will accept the proposed confidentiality/availability choices.","body":{"findings":"CLOSE-01,CLOSE-02,CLOSE-03,CLOSE-04","head":"499dc13e3ef05f8a30f844df225dc17ce4162297","no_git_artifact":"true","scope":"bounded P1 G1-G5 closure readiness; proposed P1e; no adoption or activation"}}